    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a structured address form to owner creation (`POST /api/owners`) while keeping the flat `address` field backward-compatible.\n\n**`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** (drives the generated DTOs)\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` string properties to `OwnerFields` (both optional). These flow into both `OwnerFieldsDto` (request) and `OwnerDto` (response).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list (it's now conditionally supplied), and rewrote its description to document that on read it is the composed, normalized address.\n\n**`model/Owner.java`**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns with getters/setters. `getAddress()` continues to return the stored (now composed) address, so every consumer that reads the address transparently gets the structured value.\n\n**`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 rewrote `normalizeAddress`:\n- Prefers the structured form whenever `addressLine1` normalizes to a non-blank value: normalizes both lines, stores them back, and composes `address` as the normalized `addressLine1` + a single space + normalized `addressLine2` (only when line 2 is present).\n- Otherwise falls back to the flat `address`, clearing the structured lines.\n- Returns `false` (\u2192 `400 Bad Request`) only when neither form supplies an address. City remains required, so validity is \"address in either form, plus city.\"\n\nBecause the composed value is stored into `address`, the household hash, postcode validation, and locality (which key off `postcode`/`city`/`customerCode`) and anything reading `getAddress()` all operate on the structured data when present, flat otherwise.\n\n**DB schemas** (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`)\n- Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to the `owners` table.\n\n**Test result:** `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`, covering both the structured-address normalization/composition test and the flat-address backward-compatibility test.",
